    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0098",
      "sequence": 953,
      "meaning": "Angered by the enemy’s multiplying heads and arms, the foremost bears and monkeys—Aṅgada, Hanumān, Nala, Nīla, Sugrīva and Dvivida—attack with trees and mountains. Some tear Rāvaṇa’s body with nails, others strike with kicks; Nala and Nīla climb upon his heads and rip his foreheads. Unable to seize the pair as they move over his hands like two bees through a lotus grove, Rāvaṇa finally catches and dashes them down. He then takes up ten bows, wounds the monkeys, knocks Hanumān and others unconscious and rejoices at nightfall. Jāmbavān charges with bears bearing trees and mountains; when Rāvaṇa hurls warriors to earth, the bear-king sees his army’s ruin and kicks him squarely in the chest.",
